    <h2>My Experience</h2>
    <p>We're an Asian family, so I'll admit I had some doubts when I first got my TM7. I expected it to be more of a Western-style appliance—great for soups, sauces and purées, but maybe not something that would really fit the way we cook and the food we grew up eating.</p>
    <p>I was pleasantly surprised by how wrong I was.</p>
    <p>I've made everything from congee, curries and dumpling fillings to sticky rice and mango desserts, and I've discovered that the TM7 can handle so many of the flavours and dishes our family loves. I even recently made stir-fried cabbage and sautéed shrimp, and both turned out surprisingly well. Does it give you the smoky wok hay you get from a real wok? No. Nothing really replaces that. But when I'm looking for a quick, easy meal, it's honestly pretty darn good.</p>
    <p>One of the things I love most is the consistency. I used to second-guess myself with certain dishes—Did I cook this long enough? Is the temperature right? Did I add enough seasoning? With the TM7, so much of that guesswork disappears. The guided recipes make it easy to get a good result, whether I'm cooking or one of my daughters decides to take over.</p>
    <p>And that's probably been my biggest surprise: the TM7 has changed who cooks in our house. My daughters went from watching me cook to choosing recipes and making them themselves, from start to finish. Even my mother-in-law can use it, since there are recipes available in Chinese, which makes it much more accessible for our family.</p>
    <p>I also think the size is worth mentioning. I don't think the TM7 is designed for a really large family. I'd say a family of 4–6 is where it makes the most sense depending on what you're making. If you're regularly feeding a much larger crowd, you may find yourself cooking in batches.</p>
    <p>If you're thinking about getting one, give yourself a week or two to get comfortable with the touchscreen, settings and attachments. There is a small learning curve, but once you get the hang of it, it becomes very intuitive.</p>
    <p>For me, the TM7 isn't about replacing every other way I cook. I still love my wok, and there are definitely things I'd rather cook the traditional way. What I love is that it has made everyday cooking easier, less stressful, and something the whole family can actually participate in.</p>
    <p>That's what has made the TM7 feel like more than just another appliance in our kitchen. It's become something our whole family actually uses.</p>
  </div>
</div>

